Avoid Emscripten's slow client-index upload path for polygon-mode lines.

gl4es emulates glPolygonMode(GL_LINE) by constructing a temporary edge index
array and issuing glDrawElements. With Emscripten FULL_ES2 and client-side
vertex arrays, every such draw uploads the indices and scans them in
JavaScript to discover the vertex range it must also upload. Immediate-mode
renderlists make that happen for every list, every frame; many small line
draws spend tens of milliseconds in ELEMENT_ARRAY_BUFFER bufferSubData calls.

For the GLES2+ path, preserve gl4es's existing edge generation but expand all
enabled attributes in edge-index order and issue glDrawArrays(GL_LINES).
Immediate-mode lists use temporary client arrays, trading modest extra bytes
and CPU copies for Emscripten's much cheaper client-array upload path.
Compiled display lists cache both the expanded CPU arrays and a VBO so the
expansion and upload happen once rather than regressing their persistent
buffer path.

The expansion retains vertices, normals, primary/secondary colors, fog
coordinates, and every texture-coordinate set. A missing vertex array or any
allocation failure falls back to the original indexed draw. Transient line
arrays do not overwrite a display list's existing VBO decision.

The expanded data is a derived cache paired with ind_lines. Stored display
lists own it until deletion or recompilation. append_calllist clears both
derived pointers on its shallow copy so the called list builds an independent
cache and cannot double-free the source list's data. The cache carries a
one-time memory cost proportional to generated edge vertices and enabled
attributes; immediate lists retain the per-frame allocation behavior measured
at 60 FPS by gl-repl.

GLES1 keeps the indexed path because it may synthesize final colors and
texture coordinates later in draw_renderlist.

Applied by scripts/web-deps.sh after cloning the pinned gl4es checkout.
